<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vellore</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vellore is a city in the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, known for its sixteenth-century fort built under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>. In the empire's final decades it served, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragiri">Chandragiri</a>, as a royal seat of the Aravidu kings. The Vellore Mutiny of 1806, in which Indian soldiers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> rose against their British officers, broke out in the fort.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vellore.jpg" alt="Vellore" width="320" height="169" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karthik Easvur · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Javadi_Hills.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Vellore lies on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palar-river">Palar River</a> in the Palar basin, surrounded by the Javadi Hills, an extension of the Eastern Ghats, about 135 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a>. It is the administrative headquarters of Vellore district. According to one legend the name comes from the many babul trees, called velan trees locally, that surrounded the region; another story says the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartikeya">Murugan</a>, with the epithet Velan, came to the place in anger. Because of its importance and its position relative to Madras it has also been called the Second Madras.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The recorded history of Vellore goes back to the ninth century, as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> inscription in the Annamalaiyar Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruvannamalai">Tiruvannamalai</a> shows. Many dynasties dominated it, among them the Pallava dynasty, the Medieval Cholas, the Rashtrakuta dynasty, the Sambuvaraya chieftains, Vijayanagar, the Nawabs of the Carnatic and the British. The fort and the Jalakandeswarar Temple inside it date from the Vijayanagara period; because records are lacking, the fort's exact date of building is not known. The Aravidus lost Vellore to the Bijapur Sultan before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> captured it in 1676 after a siege of four and a half months. It passed to the British in 1760, and the fort withstood <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a>'s siege from 1780 to 1782.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> was killed at Sringapatam, the British exiled his surviving children and their households to Vellore Fort, where they took part in the mutiny of 1806. In 1805 the new commander of the Madras Army, General Sir John Cradock, had approved regulations on uniform and appearance: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> were forbidden religious marks on the forehead on duty, Muslims had to shave their beards, and a new headdress with a leather cockade offended upper-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> sepoys, for whom leather was a taboo. Many sepoys came to believe their officers meant to convert them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Vellore Fort, a Monument of National Importance managed by the Archaeological Survey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, has a broad moat, granite ramparts broken by round towers, and within it a church, a mosque and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>'s family and the last king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, Vikrama Rajasinha, were held there as prisoners. The Christian Medical College and Hospital, founded in 1900 by the American medical missionary Ida S. Scudder, has grown into an institution of international standing. Vellore is also a centre of the leather industry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vellore” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the <b>Vellore</b> Mutiny of 1806 the three presidencies' armies numbered 154,500, one of the largest standing armies in the world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Military</a></li></ul></section>
